📚 AEM Learning — Servlet & ResourceResolver

📅 Day 26 — AEM Practice

Today I learned about AEM Servlets, ResourceResolver, Services, and System Users.

🔹 1. What is a Servlet?

A Servlet is used to handle an HTTP request in AEM and generate a response.

Example:

Browser
   ↓
/reen/hello
   ↓
Sling Servlet
   ↓
Java Logic
   ↓
Response

🔹 2. Types of AEM Servlets

There are mainly two common ways to register a servlet:

- Resource Type Servlet
- Path Servlet

Resource Type:

@Component(
    service = Servlet.class,
    property = {
        "sling.servlet.resourceTypes=laptop/components/hello",
        "sling.servlet.methods=GET"
    }
)

Path:

"sling.servlet.paths=/reen/hello"

🔹 3. ResourceResolver

"ResourceResolver" is used to access resources inside the AEM repository.

It can be used to:

- Get a resource
- Read properties
- Navigate child resources
- Access repository content

Example:

Resource resource = resourceResolver.getResource("/content/laptop");

🔹 4. ResourceResolver vs Resource

ResourceResolver → helps us access/search repository resources.

Resource → represents a particular resource/node-like item in AEM.

ResourceResolver
       ↓
/content/laptop
       ↓
Resource
       ↓
Properties

🔹 5. System User

I also learned why a system user is created.

A system user is a non-human AEM user used by backend code to access repository resources securely.

Example:

laptop-systemuser
       ↓
Permissions
       ↓
/content/laptop
       ↓
AEM Service
       ↓
ResourceResolver

The important idea is:

«We should not give backend code unnecessary administrator access. Instead, we give a system user only the permissions it needs.»

🧠 What I understood today

- Servlet handles requests.
- ResourceResolver accesses repository resources.
- Resource represents a particular repository resource.
- Services contain reusable backend logic.
- System users provide controlled repository access.
- Permissions/ACLs decide what a system user can access.

